# Training stack — compile ON LINUX (Colab/DGX) so the CUDA wheels resolve.
#
# torch is intentionally NOT listed below. pip has no reliable way to prefer
# a --extra-index-url over the default PyPI index when both publish a
# satisfying version (a real pip limitation, not a version-pin problem — we
# hit this twice: an exact pin and a >=-range both silently resolved to
# PyPI's CPU-only wheel instead of the CUDA one). Workflow per platform:
#
#   1. Install the correct CUDA torch build manually, once:
#        Colab (cu124, x86_64):
#          pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu124
#        DGX Spark (GB10, Blackwell sm_121, aarch64, cu130):
#          pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu130
#
#   2. Compile the lock (transformers/accelerate pull torch in transitively,
#      so pip-compile will still resolve *some* torch version from plain
#      PyPI here — that's expected, ignore it):
#        pip-compile --generate-hashes --output-file=requirements/train-linux-cu130.txt requirements/train.in
#
#   3. Delete the transitively-resolved `torch==...` entry (and its `--hash`
#      continuation lines) from the generated .txt before installing — it's
#      PyPI's CPU wheel and would overwrite the CUDA build from step 1:
#        python3 -c "
#        import re, pathlib
#        p = pathlib.Path('requirements/train-linux-cu130.txt')
#        p.write_text(re.sub(r'^torch==[^\n]*(\n {4}[^\n]*)*\n', '', p.read_text(), flags=re.M))
#        "
#      (or just open the file and remove that block by hand — it's the
#      `torch==...` line plus its indented `--hash=`/`# via` continuation lines)
#
#   4. pip install -r requirements/train-linux-cu130.txt
#      transformers/peft/etc. declare torch as an unpinned dependency, so this
#      will not touch the already-installed CUDA torch as long as its line is
#      gone from the lock. Don't add "torch" back into this .in file.
